"zefciu" <zefirek@Speacock.Pau.Apoznan.Mpl> wrote:

> I am a total noob (never got further than VoD),

Don't feel bad, I've been playing since 1987,
haven't made it to the VOD in perhaps two years now.
You're doing very well.

> 2. Got 4 pets: a dog, a housecat, a warhorse, a
> chameleon.

The warhorse is a good pet, but dies easily from
starvation, since only a few monsters drop corpses
the horse will eat; use your BoH to stash lichen
corpses and fruit, feed your horse occasionally; if
you run out of veggies, and the horse gets weak from
hunger, it will even eat a food ration.

Chameleons seem fun as pets, but sooner or later
turn into something pitiful and are best abandoned
or converted.

> 3. Skilled in scalpel (able to advance but saving
> slots), expert in unihorn (currently used), still
> basic in healing spells (maybe I should cast them
> more just to get rid of these ca 20% failure
> rates?)

Absolutely. You can cast them at your pets, and a
mix of frequent stethescope use on your pets, plus
casting plain healing on them (you can cast that
three times for every one time you can cast extra
healing) will give you lots of healing practice.  If
your PW builds up, heal yourself or a pet even when
not necessary.

> 4. Just got a BoH from a Sokoban.

Bless it, that doubles its effectiveness; if you
have another sack, keep a potion of holy water there
for reblessing the BoH later when you meet monsters
who can curse your gear. Keep in mind that even a
blessed BoH shouldn't be used as a garbage stash.
Beware as a newbie, that there are several ways to
destroy a BoH, don't do them.

Don't break the habit of constantly reviewing your
inventory and discarding less useful stuff, or
you'll _still_ find yourself burdened and thus slow,
far too often.

> I practise throwing stuff like daggers, but find
> it unsufficient.

Keep at it. The goal is to gather around 20 daggers,
not much more than that because they are very heavy
in quantities. Get elvish if you can, but they're
scarcer, otherwise plain, then, once you have a
stack to enchant all at once, enchant them right up
to +6 or +7, bless them, and be expert in daggers.
Blessed gauntlets of dexterity, enchanted a few
times, improve your hit rate, same with the ring of
dexterity.

Elvish daggers ones won't rust, ordinary ones need
to be rustproofed as an extra step.  That
combination will do an awesome amount of damage, so
much that many here recommend throwing daggers even
in melee.

[That you _can_ throw daggers in melee is IMO a bug,
real life melee stuff _should_ happen too fast for
you to pull, palm, and throw four daggers, but ...]

> On the other hand, I never saw a healer as a
> melee character, so am confused a bit.

Melee for healers as for wizards, is something best
avoided whenever possible, your PC has better things
to do, including running away screaming. Don't ever
confront a monster you can avoid confronting by any
of a large numbers of tricks: dance around your pet,
lead the monster through a pit or trap [the monster
movement algorithm is a lot more primative than your
pets, monsters will cheerfully walk, in line of
march even, into a spiked pit], run around in
circles until your pet catches up to kill the
monster, keep one big pet on a leash for monster
killing duty, etc.

As someone else answered, your pets are very
important as a healer. So is getting enough exercise
to get your strength improved up to your racial
limit. Consider pushing boulders a lot even after
Sokoban if you still need to advance strength.
Consider using a leash or a magic whistle to move
pets onto polymorph traps, to see if you can make
your current pets fiercer, especially the chameleon.

Since you get one free stethescope use for every
single turn in the game, monitor your pets closely
during battles, help them out with healing spells.

> What weapon policy should I choose and what other
> advices could you give me?

The unihorn for melee and daggers for ranged combat
should last a long time if you enchant and bless
them well; save some enhancement slots for buffing
up your use of an artifact weapon from Hermes, if
you happen to get one.

Once you have a decent weapon, the scalpel is IMO a
wasted inventory slot.

Remember that you already have a wand of sleep IDed
at the beginning of the game and they are fairly
common; use them to get out of tough situations,
better yet to avoid getting into tough situations,
but not on sleep-proof monsters like elves. Zapping
the first monster in a row coming down a hallway
with sleep stops them _all_ until your pet(s) can
arrive on site to mop up the mob. I've found /oSleep
_very_ useful in avoiding gaining XPs I want my pets
to gain instead.

Others will add more useful stuff, that's about all
I have to offer.

